# 47 (found while fixing 44, and predating it).
#
# FIXED 2026-08-28, both halves. When a list meets a peer that is not a
# list, no ELEMENT is party to the refusal -- the conflict belongs to
# the container. Go named a member: `$.a.2` here, where TypeScript named
# `$.a`. Same code and same verdict in both, so only a caller navigating
# by path was misled -- which is every agent reading `.findings[].path`.
#
# ListVal.Unify drives its element loop through `ctx.slot` and only the
# in-branch paths restored it, so a stale element slot survived into the
# not-a-list branch and makeNilErr's slot-extension stamped it. One
# line: restore the container's own slot before the refusal.
#
# MapVal had the IDENTICAL bug and it had never been reported -- it was
# found by looking for the twin once the list cause was understood. Its
# repro is the companion file, container-conflict-member-path-map.aon.
#
# Pinned by test/spec/container-path.tsv. Those had to be `vet` rows,
# not `err` rows: `err` matches its expectation as a SUBSTRING, and a
# container's path is a PREFIX of every member path beneath it, so
# `at path $.a` passes against `at path $.a.2` -- the exact answer the
# row forbids. The rest of the message is byte-identical between the
# two, so nothing else in it discriminates. A `vet` row compares the
# finding field by field, where `path` is matched exactly. Reverting the
# fix fails the three vet rows and leaves all four errc rows passing.
#
# expected: both ports report path $.a
# actual:   both ports report path $.a (was $.a.2 in Go)
# run: node ../../ts/bin/aontu.js container-conflict-member-path.aon
a: [1, 2, 3]
a: "str"
